"# Conversation history of an agent\n\nUse aos-agent history to list, delete, summarize or replace messages in one agent's conversation.\n\n```sh\naos-agent history -t NAME ls            # the last 20 messages, one per line: LINE, ROLE, TEXT (cut at 200 characters; -l for full text); a first line says how many earlier ones are not shown\naos-agent history -t NAME ls -a         # every message\naos-agent history -t NAME ls -j 5-      # lines 5 to the end, as JSON\naos-agent history -t NAME ls -n 3      # the last 3 messages (-n -3 is the same)\naos-agent history -t NAME rm 3-4\naos-agent history -t NAME summarize 1-6 \"User said their favorite color is blue.\"\naos-agent history -t NAME set 2 '{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"...\"}'\n```\n\nIn `summarize RANGE TEXT` and `set LINE JSON`, `-` reads TEXT or JSON from stdin. `summarize` writes one message that reads `[aos] summary: lines A-B, N messages. TEXT` (role `user` unless `--role`); `set` writes the message as given.\n\nNo action means `ls`. A range is `N`, `A-B` or `A-` (A to the end). For the last K messages give `-n K` (or `--last K`) instead of a range: `ls -n 3`, `rm -n 2`, `summarize -n 2 TEXT`; `-n -3` means the same as `-n 3`. `--target NAME` defaults to `$AOS_AGENT`, else the agent whose stack is the current directory (aos/cli/flags.json#/target).\n\nSome messages have an `aos` key besides `role` and `content`: `{\"letter\": NAME}` on a message that came from a letter, `{\"answer_to\": [NAME, ...]}` on a final answer. That is how `aos-agent ask` and `listen --letter` find the answer to one letter. `ls` does not show it; `ls -j` does. `set` writes the message as given, so copy the old `aos` key into it to keep the match; `summarize` writes a message without one.\n\nRemoved or replaced messages are kept in `history_removed.jsonl` next to the history; `ask` and `listen --letter` still find an answer there. `--keep FILE` puts them elsewhere; `--no-keep` drops them (asks first, also for rm, summarize and set; `-y` skips the question).\n\nPause a running agent first: `aos-agent ctl pause NAME`. A running fsm agent uses the change from its next request. An eca agent (claude, codex) starts a new conversation on its next turn and carries the changed record over.\n\nErrors: `TargetMissing`, `HistoryMissing`, `RangeBad` (bad range, or an empty history). Also `HistoryBad` (history unreadable), `MessageBad` (set: not one JSON object), `WriteFailed`.\n\nMore flags: run `aos-agent history --help`.\n\nAn agent changes its own history with tool `self_history`: see `aos/me.json#/self_history`.\n"
